  "summary": "P1 SECURITY \u2013 likely account compromise via MFA fatigue, VIP (Marcus Reyes, CFO, user_id 2). Reported: several unrequested Duo pushes early this morning; user denied them but may have approved one by accident. Findings in sign-in logs: 2026-10-10 08:10, 08:11, 08:12 UTC VPN attempts from Singapore, IP 192.0.2.77 (hosting network), result mfa_denied; 08:14 UTC (Sat 04:14 ET) VPN SUCCESS from same IP with Duo push approved. So the attacker had the correct password and got VPN access. Normal pattern: New York, residential 198.51.100.22 / corporate 203.0.113.10, weekdays ~09:14 ET. Duo is enrolled on user's phone (enrolled 2025-12-15). Actions queued and awaiting tech approval: block_sign_in (approval #1), reset_password (approval #2; give temp password by phone only after callback verification). Still needed: approve block/reset now; kill active VPN/M365 sessions and revoke tokens; investigate what the attacker did from 192.0.2.77 after 08:14 UTC (VPN access to internal resources, finance systems, mailbox rules/forwarding, possible BEC/wire fraud risk since user is CFO); check Duo for push activity and consider enabling number matching; look into how the password was exposed; check laptop BL-LT-0102.",
  "reason": "KB-029 says always escalate for security compromises; ticket is P1, VIP, and logs confirm a successful attacker sign-in"
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Marcus, thanks for reporting this so quickly. You were right to be worried. Around 4:10 this morning (New York time), someone overseas tried to sign in to your account several times. That's why you got the pushes. You denied three of them, but one was approved at 4:14 and the person got a VPN connection. So we're treating your account as compromised. What happens next: a technician has been asked to approve blocking sign-ins to your account, and to approve a password reset. Once they do, you'll get a call at the number we have on file to confirm it's you, and they'll give you a temporary password over the phone. You won't get it by email. Our security team is also looking into what was accessed. Until then: please don't approve any Duo push you didn't start yourself, even if it seems urgent. Deny it and tell us. Please also be careful with any unusual payment or wire requests, and let us know if anything in your email or accounts looks wrong. We'll be in touch shortly.",
  "internal_note": "P1 security, VIP (CFO). Logs show an MFA fatigue attack: 3 denied VPN pushes from Singapore, hosting IP 192.0.2.77, at 08:10-08:12 UTC 2026-10-10, then a successful VPN sign-in from the same IP at 08:14 UTC (04:14 ET) after a push was approved. The attacker had a valid password. The user's normal sign-ins are from NY residential/corporate IPs on weekdays around 09:14 ET. Duo is enrolled on the user's phone. Queued for approval: block_sign_in (#1) and reset_password (#2). Verify by callback before giving the temp password, by phone only. Escalated to Tier 2 (escalation #1) for session/token revocation and to investigate what was done after 08:14 UTC (internal access, finance systems, mailbox rules/forwarding, BEC risk) and on laptop BL-LT-0102. KB-031, which KB-004 refers to, was not found in search, so I followed KB-029.",
